  "query_preview": "..\nIssued at 1231 PM MDT Fri Sep 25 2026\n\nThe upper level trough that had been to our west drawing up\nmoisture into the region most of this week is finally on the move\nand will sweep across the area this evening. Aloft drier air was\nmoving into the region so that will help limit our thunderstorms.\nBut we will have enough moisture and lift associated with the\ntrough to give us a few afternoon and evening thunderstorms. And\nwith the trough swinging through a few storms could approach\nsevere limits with wind and hail. The biggest threat for severe\nweather will be in Hudspeth county where enough moisture will\ncombine with 2 to 3K CAPE (Convective Available Potential Energy)\nto help give the storms an extra kick.\n\nFor Saturday and Sunday we will see a short wave ridge move across\nNew Mexico and that will keep us generally dry and with our high\ntemperatures both days running very near seasonal averages. But\nthings begin to change on Monday. The models are beginning to come\ninto a better agreement on the track of Hurricane Polo. By Sunday\nafternoon Hurricane Polo will be off the coast of the Baja of\nCalifornia. Early on Monday it will begin to recurve to the north\nand eventually toward the northeast. At the same time an upper\nlevel trough will be dropping into southern California. Initially\non Monday, the trough begins to draw abundant tropical moisture\ninto the region. Precipitable water values (PW's) on Sunday will\nbe a little below a one inch, but by Monday afternoon PW's will \nrange from 1.4 to 1.6 which is well above average. On Tuesday the \nupper level trough will continue to pull the moisture associated \nwith Polo into the region and it will draw the remnants of Polo \nnortheastward into northern Mexico. It is looking more and more \nlikely that we will see threat for heavy rain and flash flooding \nMonday afternoon through Tuesday night. Keep an eye on the \nforecast this weekend as we update the timing and amounts of \npotential rainfall. On Wednesday the main upper level trough will\nswing across the region keeping the threat for rain in the \nforecast. After Wednesday we will see a distinct drying trend and \nthe end of next week is looking very nice. As you might expect \nwith the clouds and rain we will see below average high \ntemperatures for the start of next week. Then with the drying \ntrend, temperatures will rebound back closer to normal for the end\nof next week.",
